void copyBytes(void* destination, const void* source, size_t numBytes) {
    // Check if any of the arguments are null or invalid
    if (destination == NULL || source == NULL || numBytes <= 0) {
        return;
    }

    // Make sure that the number of bytes to copy is within the bounds of the buffer
    if (numBytes > 256) {
        numBytes = 256;
    }

    // Copy the bytes from the source to the destination
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numBytes; i++) {
        ((char*)destination)[i] = ((const char*)source)[i];
    }
}
```
In this example, the function takes three arguments: `destination`, which is a pointer to the destination memory location where the bytes should be copied; `source`, which is a pointer to the source memory location from where the bytes should be copied; and `numBytes`, which is the number of bytes to copy.

The function first checks if any of the arguments are null or invalid, and returns immediately if so. Then, it makes sure that the number of bytes to copy is within the bounds of the buffer (in this case, 256 bytes). If the number of bytes to copy is greater than the size of the buffer, it sets the number of bytes to the maximum allowed value (256).

Next, the function uses a loop to iterate over each byte in the source memory location and copies it to the corresponding position in the destination memory location. The casts to `char*` are used to treat the pointers as arrays of characters, which allows us to access and modify individual bytes.']

